cleanup continues for residents in the  lake sahara apartments in fact this is  video showing the storm damage caused  last month now nearly two weeks later  there's still more to do victoria saha  joins us live from that complex after  hearing from people living there  yes denise it looks like there's still a  lot of work that still needs to be done  from that storm damage and take a look  over here big pieces of trees still just  hanging out in the courtyard waiting to  be moved and a lot of these apartments  still have signs asking residents to not  enter  the damage that monsoon season brings  could be unpredictable  but seeing what's left from the july  storms shows us just how strong mother  nature could be we walked around the  lake sahara apartments and saw work that  is still being done to clear out the  damage i think i counted  eight trees totaled it were down on the  property william salazar lives at the  lake sahara apartments he says he was  one of 16 families that had to be  evacuated after a tree fell onto his  building after  leaving here we went to  motel 6 stayed there for a couple of  nights and then one of our friends from  work  our co-worker he  let us come over to his house and bring  our animals he says he didn't know when  he would be allowed to live in his  apartment again but came back to empty  out his fridge since the power was  turned off all in all  with me my wife and our roommate we have  spent close to two thousand dollars  with  the amount that you know just taking  care of  business and feeding everybody and as  monsoon season continues to keep us on  the edge salazar says living in the top  floor has become a worry for him there's  been no end to the stress we've just you  know one blow after another take it day  by day  just recently we did actually get an  update from salazar he said that his  power was finally back on nearly after  two weeks so he was allowed to move back  to his apartment today i did reach out  to the apartment complex to find out how  many residents are still without their  home but i'm still waiting to hear back  victoria zaha live local now
 

